<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian II of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1481">1481</a>–<a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Christian the Tyrant · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> was king of Denmark and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1513 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1520 to 1521, the last monarch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a> to rule all three kingdoms. After reconquering Sweden he had some eighty nobles and clergy executed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm-bloodbath">Stockholm Bloodbath</a> of November 1520, provoking the revolt that made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> king. Deposed by the Danish nobles in 1523, he spent his last decades in captivity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-ii-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Christian II of Denmark" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_(I)_-_Bildnis_Christians_II.,_K%C3%B6nig_von_D%C3%A4nemark_(MbK,_Leipzig).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christian was born at Nyborg Castle on 1 July 1481, the son of King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and Christina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. He took part in his father's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1497 and in the Dano-Swedish War of 1501–1512. In 1506 he became viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, where he kept control but quarrelled with the privy council by trying to reduce the nobility's traditional influence.</p><p>He succeeded his father in 1513. In 1515 he married Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, a granddaughter of the Holy Roman Emperor Maximilian I. His mistress Dyveke Sigbritsdatter remained with him until her death in 1517, and her mother, Sigbrit Willoms, became his chief counsellor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Believing that the magnate Torben Oxe had poisoned Dyveke, Christian had him tried by a common jury, not the Council of State, and executed in November 1517, which deepened the division between king and aristocracy. His attempts to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from the regent Sten Sture the Younger failed in 1517 and 1518 but succeeded in 1520 with French, German and Scottish mercenaries. Christian was crowned king of Sweden in November.</p><p>On 8 and 9 November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm-bloodbath">Stockholm Bloodbath</a> took place: after an ecclesiastical court condemned Sture's followers for heresy, they were executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> castle, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Skara and Strängnäs. The killings provoked a rising led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>. In Denmark Christian issued a Town Law and a Land Law in 1521, strengthening tradesmen and peasants at the nobility's expense and allowing clergy to marry. Nobles and bishops saw them as a threat, and after rebellion began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> the crown was offered on 20 January 1523 to his uncle Frederick.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Christian left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in April 1523 and lived in exile at Lier in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where he corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and for a time became a Lutheran. By 1531 he was Catholic again and sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, but failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akershus-fortress">Akershus Castle</a> and accepted a promise of safe conduct from Frederick I. It was broken, and he was held for 27 years, first at Sønderborg and from 1549 at Kalundborg, where he was treated as a nobleman. His supporters were defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counts-feud">Count's Feud</a> of 1536. He died at Kalundborg in January 1559 and is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> as Christian the Tyrant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christian II of Denmark” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Christian II</b> reconquered Sweden in 1520 and executed some eighty nobles and clergy in the Stockholm Bloodbath, provoking a revolt led by Gustav Vasa, who was elected king of Sweden in 1523.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nyborg, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Kalundborg, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.31,10.79&amp;year=1531" aria-label="Nyborg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4786 275 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christian II of Denmark"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5236 545h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5236 545h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.31,10.79&amp;year=1531">What was here in 1531? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
